# Shared Components - Cursor Rules

## Overview

This package contains shared components, hooks, stores, and utilities used across all games in the monorepo.

## Architecture & Tech Stack

-   **Framework**: Next.js (Pages Router)
-   **Language**: TypeScript
-   **State Management**: Zustand stores
-   **Styling**: Tailwind CSS + SCSS modules
-   **Real-time**: Socket.IO
-   **Testing**: Jest + React Testing Library

## File Structure

```
packages/shared/src/
├── components/           # Reusable UI components
│   ├── game-components/ # Game-specific shared components
│   ├── page-components/ # Page-level components
│   └── round-base/      # Round-based game components
├── stores/              # Zustand stores
│   ├── game-store/      # Game state management
│   └── betting-store/   # Betting state management
├── react-hooks/         # Custom hooks
├── helpers/             # Utility functions
├── constants/           # App constants
└── react-contexts/      # React contexts
```

## Coding Standards

### Component Patterns

```typescript
// Functional components with TypeScript
const SharedComponent: React.FC<Props> = ({ prop1, prop2 }) => {
    const gameData = useGameStore((state) => state.playerResultBoard);

    return <div className="tailwind-classes">{/* Component content */}</div>;
};

// Custom hooks
const useSharedHook = () => {
    const [state, setState] = useState();

    useEffect(() => {
        // Hook logic
    }, []);

    return { state };
};
```

### State Management

-   Use Zustand stores for global state
-   Avoid React Context for game data
-   Store `playerResultBoard` in `GameStateSlice`
-   Use `registerGameConfigEvent` for websocket data

### Socket.IO Integration

-   Event names must match exactly
-   Listen for both `gameName` and `websocketEventName` variants
-   Use `GameMessageHandler` for broadcasting events
-   Register events in stores via `registerEvent`

### Error Handling

-   Wrap API calls in try-catch blocks
-   Provide user-friendly error messages
-   Log errors for debugging
-   Handle websocket connection failures gracefully

### Performance Optimization

-   Use `React.memo` for expensive components
-   Minimize re-renders with proper dependency arrays
-   Lazy load non-critical components
-   Optimize images with `next/image`

### Testing Requirements

-   Write unit tests for all utilities and hooks
-   Test websocket event handling
-   Mock Zustand stores in tests
-   Test error scenarios and edge cases

## Common Patterns

### Zustand Store Pattern

```typescript
// Store slice pattern
export interface GameStateSlice {
    playerResultBoard?: PlayerResultBoard;
    roundHistory: Round[];
    // ... other properties

    registerGameConfigEvent: (message: any) => void;
    setGameState: (message: any) => void;
}

// Store usage
const useGameStore = create<GameStateSlice>((set, get) => ({
    // ... implementation
}));
```

### Socket.IO Pattern

```typescript
// Socket connection pattern
export function InitSocketIO(socketInfo: SocketInfo, session: any, gameName: string, gameMessageHandler: GameMessageHandler) {
    const socket = io(socketUri, socketOption);

    if (socket) {
        socket.on(gameName, (message) => {
            gameMessageHandler.broadcastGameEvent(message);
            useGameStore.getState().registerEvent(message);
        });
    }

    return { socket };
}
```

### Event Handler Pattern

```typescript
// Event handler pattern
export class GameMessageHandler {
    broadcastGameEvent = (message: any) => {
        // Broadcast to all registered handlers
        this.eventHandlers.forEach((handler) => {
            handler(message);
        });
    };
}
```

## Security Considerations

-   Validate all user inputs
-   Sanitize data before displaying
-   Use secure defaults for Socket.IO connections
-   Implement proper authentication checks

## Common Issues & Solutions

### Websocket Data Not Displaying

-   Check event name matching
-   Verify `GameMessageHandler` registration
-   Ensure Zustand store is receiving events via `registerEvent`

### State Not Updating

-   Check Zustand store implementation
-   Verify event registration
-   Ensure proper dependency arrays in `useEffect`

### Performance Issues

-   Use `React.memo` for expensive components
-   Check for unnecessary re-renders
-   Optimize bundle size
-   Use lazy loading

## Development Guidelines

-   Follow TypeScript strict mode
-   Use descriptive variable names
-   Add comprehensive logging for debugging
-   Write self-documenting code
-   Update tests when adding features
-   Follow Next.js best practices

## File Naming Conventions

-   Components: PascalCase (e.g., `GameComponent.tsx`)
-   Hooks: camelCase with use prefix (e.g., `useGameHook.ts`)
-   Stores: camelCase (e.g., `game-store.ts`)
-   Utilities: camelCase (e.g., `game-helper.ts`)
-   Types: PascalCase (e.g., `GameTypes.ts`)

## Import Patterns

```typescript
// Shared components
import { Component } from "@shared/components/component";

// Game-specific
import { GameComponent } from "../game-component";

// External libraries
import { io } from "socket.io-client";
```

